# Tenant wiring

Each external repo that deploys into this cluster gets one YAML file here plus a
matching entry in `kustomization.yaml` (Kustomize requires explicit resource
listings). The file carries the tenant's secret store, Flux `GitRepository`,
reconciliation `ServiceAccount` + RoleBindings, and top-level `Kustomization`.

Removal is the inverse: delete the file and its `kustomization.yaml` line. Flux
prunes the Kustomization (and everything it created, including ESO-owned
Secrets), the GitRepository, the secret store and the namespace.

## The tenant side of the contract

A tenant repo needs a `kubernetes/flux/` directory that `kustomize build`
resolves cleanly, and CI that lints it. **weisssrv-app-template** generates
exactly that — manifests, lint + kubeconform + secret-scan pipeline, and the
namespace/PSS conventions this cluster expects. Point new tenants at it rather
than at a hand-rolled tree.

Two things the tenant must match, and one this repo owes them:

- **Path.** The `Kustomization.spec.path` below is `./kubernetes/flux`; the
  app-template puts manifests there. Change one and change the other.
- **Namespace.** The tenant's manifests must be namespace-agnostic (the
  `targetNamespace` on the Kustomization sets it) or hard-code the namespace this
  file creates. Mixing the two produces resources in two namespaces, only one of
  which Flux prunes.
- **Deploys happen cluster-side.** Tenant CI lints and builds; it does not
  `kubectl apply`. Flux pulls. A tenant that also deploys from CI will fight the
  reconciler.

Nothing about tenant onboarding requires the app-template — a hand-authored
`kubernetes/flux/` works identically. It just means writing the pipeline and the
conventions again.

### Tenant CI is the tenant's choice, and one option is none

**The cluster side is indifferent to a tenant's CI, whatever it is** — the deploy
is always this cluster pulling, never the tenant pushing. That is true today and
is the part that matters here.

The app-template asks for the shape up front — its `ci_shape` answer — so a
tenant is generated with the pipeline it wants rather than editing one out
afterwards:

| `ci_shape` | What the tenant repo carries | What this cluster does |
|---|---|---|
| `gitlab_selfhosted` | a `.gitlab-ci.yml` that lints and kubeconforms `kubernetes/flux/` | pulls and reconciles |
| `github` | the same gates as GitHub Actions workflows | pulls and reconciles |
| `none` | no pipeline at all | pulls and reconciles |

Shape `none` is a real option, not a degraded one: the `Kustomization` below is
the only thing that applies anything, so a tenant with no CI is deployed exactly
like a tenant with CI — it just has no pre-merge gate of its own. It does put the
whole burden on Flux being able to clone the repository, so read the private-repo
section below before choosing it for a private tenant.

### Tenant repositories are host-independent

`{{ git_host }}` is where **this cluster's** repository lives. It has nothing to
do with where a tenant's repository lives: Flux clones over plain HTTPS or SSH,
so a tenant can sit on GitHub while this cluster sits on
`{{ git_host }}`, or on a third host entirely. Copy the example's URL only if the
tenant really is on the same host — otherwise write the tenant's own:

```yaml
# same host as this cluster
url: https://{{ git_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/example-app
# a GitHub-hosted tenant, on the same cluster
url: https://github.com/<owner>/example-app
# either host, over SSH (required for a private repo authenticated by deploy key)
url: ssh://git@github.com/<owner>/example-app.git
```

### Private tenant repositories

Flux authenticates per `GitRepository`, through a Secret named by
`spec.secretRef.name`. That Secret must live in the **same namespace as the
GitRepository** (`flux-system` here — `secretRef` is namespace-local), and it is
**bootstrap state, not Flux-managed**: it is what Flux needs in order to read
git, so it cannot itself come from git.

Deploy key (preferred — scoped to one repository, and revocable there):

```bash
flux create secret git example-app-git-auth \
  --namespace=flux-system \
  --url=ssh://git@github.com/<owner>/example-app \
  --ssh-key-algorithm=ecdsa \
  --ssh-ecdsa-curve=p521
```

That prints a public key. Add it to the tenant repository as a deploy key —
**read-only**, no write access; Flux never pushes to a tenant repo — then point
the source at it:

```yaml
spec:
  url: ssh://git@github.com/<owner>/example-app.git   # ssh://, and the .git suffix
  secretRef:
    name: example-app-git-auth
```

Token instead of a key (GitHub PAT, GitLab deploy token or project access
token), when the host makes tokens easier to scope than keys:

```bash
flux create secret git example-app-git-auth \
  --namespace=flux-system \
  --url=https://github.com/<owner>/example-app \
  --username=git \
  --password=<TOKEN>
```

The `url:` on the GitRepository then stays `https://…`, with the same
`secretRef.name`. Either way, rotating the credential is re-running the command;
nothing in git changes.

## Example

```yaml
# {{ cluster_name }}/tenants/example-app.yaml
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: example-app
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: flux
    fluxcd.io/tenant: example-app
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: baseline
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
    # DELIBERATELY ABSENT: {{ internal_domain }}/cluster-secrets: "true".
    # That label is what the PLATFORM ClusterSecretStore's spec.conditions admit,
    # and it would give this tenant read access to every item in the platform
    # vault. The tenant gets its own store, below.
---
# One-time bootstrap (NOT Flux-managed), scoped to this tenant so revocation is
# independent:
#   op connect token create {{ cluster_name }}-example-app-eso \
#     --server <EXISTING_SERVER_ID> --vaults <vault>
#   kubectl -n example-app create secret generic onepassword-connect-token \
#     --from-literal=token=<CONNECT_TOKEN>
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1
kind: ClusterSecretStore
metadata:
  name: onepassword-example-app
spec:
  # Scoped to this tenant's namespace, so the NEXT tenant cannot name this store
  # and read through it. A ClusterSecretStore without conditions is usable from
  # every namespace in the cluster.
  conditions:
    - namespaces:
        - example-app
  provider:
    onepassword:
      connectHost: http://onepassword-connect.external-secrets.svc.cluster.local:8080
      vaults:
        <vault>: 1
      auth:
        secretRef:
          connectTokenSecretRef:
            name: onepassword-connect-token
            namespace: example-app
            key: token
---
apiVersion: source.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: GitRepository
metadata:
  name: example-app
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 1m
  # This host because the example tenant is co-located; a tenant on GitHub (or
  # anywhere else Flux can clone from) writes its own URL here — see
  # "Tenant repositories are host-independent" above.
  url: https://{{ git_host }}/{{ git_namespace }}/example-app
  ref:
    branch: main
  # Private repo: add `secretRef: {name: example-app-git-auth}` and create that
  # Secret with `flux create secret git` — see "Private tenant repositories"
  # above. It is bootstrap state and lives in flux-system, alongside this
  # GitRepository, because secretRef is namespace-local.
---
# Without serviceAccountName, kustomize-controller applies tenant manifests with
# its own cluster-admin credentials. The SA must live in the Kustomization's OWN
# namespace (flux-system) — kustomize-controller impersonates
# system:serviceaccount:<Kustomization namespace>:<name>. The RoleBindings then
# grant that SA rights in the tenant namespace and nowhere else.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: example-app-flux
  namespace: flux-system
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: example-app-flux-admin
  namespace: example-app
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: example-app-flux
    namespace: flux-system
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: admin
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
# Fills admin's CRD gaps — see tenant-crd-editor.yaml.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: example-app-flux-crd-editor
  namespace: example-app
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: example-app-flux
    namespace: flux-system
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: tenant-crd-editor
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
apiVersion: kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: Kustomization
metadata:
  name: example-app
  namespace: flux-system
spec:
  interval: 10m
  retryInterval: 1m
  timeout: 10m
  # Mirror the platform's apps.yaml: wait for the CRD chain so a fresh
  # bootstrap does not apply tenant CRs before ESO/cert-manager/Traefik exist.
  dependsOn:
    - name: infrastructure-configs
  serviceAccountName: example-app-flux
  sourceRef:
    kind: GitRepository
    name: example-app
  path: ./kubernetes/flux
  prune: true
  targetNamespace: example-app
  wait: true
```

## Before onboarding the first tenant

- **Never label a tenant namespace into the platform store.** The platform
  `ClusterSecretStore` (`infrastructure/configs/cluster-secret-store.yaml`)
  carries `spec.conditions` admitting only namespaces labelled
  `{{ internal_domain }}/cluster-secrets: "true"`. That condition is the only
  thing standing between a tenant and the CI runner tokens, the identity
  provider's keys and the SMTP credentials — the tenant's `admin` RoleBinding
  lets it create ExternalSecrets freely, and RBAC in its own namespace cannot
  re-impose the boundary. Adding that label to a tenant namespace hands over the
  whole platform vault in one line. Give the tenant its own store instead, as
  the example above does.
- **Vault isolation.** Even a tenant's *own* `ClusterSecretStore` can read every
  item in whatever vault it points at, so a per-tenant store over the shared
  vault only isolates the token, not the contents. Acceptable for a
  single-operator homelab; give tenants their own vault (or their own Connect
  server) otherwise. Their store also needs its own `spec.conditions` — scope it
  to the tenant namespace, or the next tenant can name it too.
- **Traefik cross-namespace refs.** The platform runs the Traefik CRD provider
  with `allowCrossNamespace: true` so app IngressRoutes can use the shared
  middlewares in the `traefik` namespace. A tenant-authored IngressRoute can
  therefore also reference another namespace's Service. Scope the provider per
  tenant, add a validating policy, or set it back to `false` before opening the
  cluster to untrusted authors.
- **Namespace ownership** is cooperative: nothing stops a tenant manifest from
  targeting a platform namespace today. An admission policy engine
  (Kyverno/OPA) is the durable fix.
